A||N||11||Monkeyman||What false firewall?????!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-20-2000||10:56 PM||||I dug around in the Monkeymobile today so I could see how much room I'd have to mount my amp and CD changer on the false firewall.  (I'd like to sink them in it a little.)  I removed the seats, removed the flat panel under the rear window, pulled the carpet back, and removed the black sticky layer of "stuff" (yuck).  I got disappointed when I didn't see a bunch of extra room back there.  I did notice what looked like a panel that was welded/glued in place.  Do I need to remove this to gain the extra room?  How much space will I gain?  How do I remove the panel?  If I have enough room, I might build an extra inch or so into the firewall.  Sinking the amp won't be a problem, but my CD changer chassis is pretty big.  Can I assume that the firewall in the Grape Ape ('85 GT) is the same as the one in the Monkeymobile ('86 SC)??||||reg||1
Z||000001||Terrybogin||06-20-2000||11:11 PM||||The false firewall is there as heat insulation from the exhaust pipe and cat. converter. It also provides some sound deadening from the exhaust. With the front side of the firewall removed, the rear panel is the exterior sheet metal in the engine compartment. Do you really want to mount audio components to this?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spyder||06-20-2000||11:26 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/subcutout.jpg">  <BR>This is the pic of the portion that I removed and fiberglassed in. The glued and spot welded in portion that you said was removed.<BR>Here are a couple of links where we have talked about this: <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001430.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001430.html</A>  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001208.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001208.html</A>  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 06-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Loki||06-20-2000||11:41 PM||||Check out my page....<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cox-internet.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.cox-internet.com/fiero</A> <P>got pics up and im working on re-writing it. Just ask and ill answer||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||06-21-2000||12:58 AM||||I'm planning on doing this same kind of install on my fiero as soon as I get it back from the shop, it may be a little tighter tho because I'm going to try and fit a set of 12's in there, I'm only 5'11" and my g/f is much shorter then me so I can stand to lose a &gt;bit&lt; of recline area. I checked it out last weekend, and I think if I angle the subs just a slight ammount I will be able to put the seat back one more notch then what I usually drive at, the only other problem that I'm seeing (well kinda) I'm not a sound engineer, and I'm not planning on going up for shows, I just like loud music in a fast car, but anyways, most speakers require so many sq ft of air to operate optimully, if I rember right, it was either Loki's or FieroSound's that needed .5 sq ft to get the best sound, mine will be a little less then that onece the 6x9s are in also. I guess it all matters on your felxibility as far as costs, time, quality. Me, I have the 12s, 6x9s, amp, and head, so costs are nill, quality, well, I've never had a "professional" quality sterio, and I don't know if I'll ever really want one, and time, well, I have to drive home July 17th, so, that's the main push!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-21-2000||10:07 AM||||Spyder--Why did you glass in that area?  I was under the assumption that no extra work was needed unless you cut into the "real" firewall.  If the dimensions are big enough, I'd like to install my amp in that hole (some kind of sound deadener (good thought, Terrybogin) then just use some self tapping metal screws to fasten it to the "real" firewall.  If I cut out the same area on the driver side (where I want the amp)  will I have to worry about accidentally cutting into the fuel lines?  I guess what I'm really trying to ask is this:  Once I cut out the spot welded/glued piece of metal, will I see another piece of metal behind that or will I see an exhaust manifold???||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-21-2000||10:08 AM||||Oh.  And can anyone give me the approximate dimensions of the hole including depth?  I'd like to make sure everything's going to fit before I cut stuff out.<P>Thanx!  You guys are great!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Spyder||06-23-2000||11:48 AM||||MM,<BR>I fiberglassed  it because it was the best way “to me” to seal it up, sealed speaker box.<BR>You will want to at least spray foam the gap that is left in between the firewalls.<BR>The cutout on the drivers side ended up being smaller than the passenger side because of the fuel lines.<BR>You will see metal (the second firewall) when you cut out that piece of metal.<BR>Like I said in earlier post, I just used a chisel and hammer to pry that section up.<BR>I don’t have the measurements with me. I will try my best to get the measurements.<BR>Also, I sent you an email, Did you get it?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-23-2000||12:17 PM||||I received an email about an unrelated topic.  I guess you didn't get my reply.  Thanx for the info about the other.  I'm still undecided.  Whenever you get a chance let me know the dimensions.  Both sides if possible as I need to see if I even have enough room for my amp and my CD changer before I start cutting.  Thanx. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spyder||06-25-2000||02:07 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cutoutpass.JPG"> <BR>This is the passenger side.<BR>The 1 inch riser, I’m not sure that’s on all cars, but that made the depth to 2 inches.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spyder||06-25-2000||02:17 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cutoutdrive.JPG"> <BR>This is the driver’s side.<BR>The left top portion is where the fuel lines are located..<P>Also note the measurements are approximate working space.<P>These areas can be cut out larger, I didn’t need them to be any larger.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:48 AM||||Exactly what I needed.  Thank you too much.  Any idea what the riser is for on the pass side?  At least I can see if my components will fit.  I have a feeling one of them will either have to be surface mounted or mounted elsewhere, but at least it's a start.  Thanx, again. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
